.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-ACCOUNT 1 "2026-10-01" "ShardPost 0.36.1" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-account \- look up an account, its quota and its shares; list the account directory; repair a directory entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl account show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl quota show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl shared list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl account directory
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl account repair
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl account list
.RB [ \-limit
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-after
.IR cursor ]
.RB [ \-state
.BR active | purging ]
.RB [ \-all ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
All but the last command read. They change nothing and are the usual
first step when someone reports a problem with an account.
.B account repair
is the exception; see below.
.SS account show
Answers the question "does this account exist, and what does the server
see?" in one place:
.TP
.B lookup
What the user database (Nauthilus) says about the name you passed:
.B canonical
(this is the account itself),
.B alias
(with the canonical account it belongs to),
.B unknown
or
.B unavailable
(the directory did not answer). Most support cases end here, because the
name in the ticket is an alias or a typo.
.TP
.B store
Whether the account has data in the store, and how many mailboxes.
.TP
.B quota
Usage and limit, in bytes and messages.
.TP
.B import lock
Whether an import or a maintenance job currently holds the account. While
it does, IMAP, POP3 and LMTP refuse the account on purpose.
.TP
.B trace
Whether a protocol trace is switched on; see
.BR shardpostctl-trace (1).
.PP
The account must be given in its canonical form; an alias is reported as
such but is not followed.
.SS quota show
Usage and limit only. The limit comes from the user database (the LDAP
attribute in the operating profile), the usage from the store's counters.
If the two look implausible, recount them with the
.B quota_recalc
job; see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1).
.SS shared list
Lists the mailboxes of other accounts this account was granted rights
on, as the client sees them under the shared prefix
.RI ( server.shared_prefix ,
by default
.IR Shared/ ).
For the other direction (who may see this account's mailboxes) use
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1).
.PP
These three commands require the role
.BR diagnose .
.SS account directory
Shows the account's entry in the account directory, the store-wide list
of accounts:
.TP
.B entry
.B active
or
.B purging
(with the lock session of the purge job), or no entry.
.TP
.B created
When the account was last created (its registry; for accounts the
backfill entered, the registry's last change).
.TP
.B registry
Whether the account's data still exists (its registry object).
.TP
.B directory
Shard count and phase of the directory.
.TP
.B last scrub
When the last batch scrub of the account ended, whether it was complete
and how many findings it had; see
.B "plan \-all \-skip-idle scrub"
in
.BR shardpostctl-job (1).
.TP
.B backfill
The completion marker of the last
.B shardpost index backfill accounts
(start, end and counts as JSON), or none.
.PP
The account is not looked up in the user database. The command requires
the role
.BR diagnose .
.SS account list
Lists the account directory: every account that has data in the store,
and the public namespace, with its state, when it was created and its
last batch scrub. The directory is read page by page in its own order
(shard, then account), not alphabetically; it is never a listing of the
storage pool. A page holds
.B \-limit
accounts (100 by default, at most 1000); the line
.I more:
names the cursor that
.B \-after
continues with, and
.B \-all
fetches every page, printing each one as it arrives.
.B \-state
keeps only the entries in that state; such a page may hold fewer
accounts while more follow. With
.B \-\-json
a page prints as one document; with
.B \-all
and
.B \-\-json
every entry prints as one JSON line, so a large directory is never held
in memory. The command requires the role
.B diagnose
and the scope of all accounts: socket callers have it, mutual-TLS
clients only with
.I all_accounts
in their configuration.
.SS account repair
Repairs the account's entry in the account directory, the store-wide
list of accounts. The purge job marks an account
.B purging
before it deletes it and removes the entry at its end; while the mark is
there and the account's data is gone, nothing can create the account
again. A purge that failed or was cancelled in between leaves the mark
behind, and the account stays unusable. The repair applies these rules:
.TP
.B purge_running
A purge job of the recorded lock session is still queued or running
(another server may continue it): nothing is changed. Wait for the job or
cancel it; see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1).
.TP
.B removed
The account was
.B purging
and its data is gone: the entry is removed, and the name can be used
again.
.TP
.B activated
The account was
.B purging
but its data still exists (the purge stopped before it deleted the
registry): the entry is
.B active
again.
.TP
.B stale_removed
The entry is
.BR active ,
older than ten minutes, and the account's data is gone: the entry is
stale (a purge by an older server version, a crash between the entry and
the creation of the account) and is removed.
.TP
.B none
Anything else: nothing to do.
.PP
The output shows the action and the state before and after. The account
is not looked up in the user database, since a purged account may no
longer exist there. The command requires the role
.BR repair .
The backfill
.RB ( "shardpost index backfill accounts" )
applies the same rules to every account.
.PP
While an account is left
.B purging
by a purge that ended, no other maintenance job can lock it \(em a second
purge included. Such a job fails at once with "run shardpostctl account
repair"; run the repair first, then plan the job again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-limit " n"
.B account list:
accounts per page.
.TP
.BI \-after " cursor"
.B account list:
continue at this cursor
.RI ( shard : account ,
from the
.I more:
line or
.I next
of the JSON).
.TP
.BI \-state " state"
.B account list:
only entries that are
.B active
or
.BR purging .
.TP
.B \-all
.B account list:
fetch every page.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON instead of a table.
.SH EXAMPLES
A user says "my mail client cannot log in":
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl account show user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check whether an address is only an alias:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl account show info@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
"Is my mailbox full?"
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl quota show user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
Before deleting an account, see what it would take away from others:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl shared list user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
A purge job failed halfway and the account now refuses every login and
delivery with "try again later":
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl job list -account user@example.org
shardpostctl account directory user@example.org
shardpostctl account repair user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
How many accounts are being purged right now?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl account list -all -state purging
.RE
.fi
.PP
Every account of the store, one JSON line each:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl account list -all --json | jq -r .account
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The account was looked up, the directory listed, or the repair ran
(whatever its action).
.TP
.B 64
Wrong usage, an invalid account name, cursor, limit or state.
.TP
.B 67
No such account.
.TP
.B 75
Temporary failure (the store did not answer); try again.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role
.B diagnose
(or
.B repair
for
.BR "account repair" ),
the account is outside its scope, the client lacks the scope of all
accounts
.RB ( "account list" ),
or the server has no account directory or job system.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-mailbox (1),
.BR shardpostctl-trace (1)
